1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,500
A simple flush.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,340
But where does it all go?

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:09,740
A dark place where creatures converge with the flotsam and jetsam.

4
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:13,020
Underground labyrinths of acrid activity.

5
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:17,860
As necessary to our survival is the food we eat and the water we drink.

6
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:21,240
Where human necessity meets cutting-edge technology.

7
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:25,240
This is where it all goes when we all go.

8
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,940
Now, Seward's on Modern Marvents.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,780
Meet Eric Teal.

10
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:44,460
Eric is going diving,

11
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,540
but he won't be seeing any exotic fish or sunken treasure.

12
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:50,880
Eric is a sewer diver.

13
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:53,640
Eric will lower himself into the muck

14
00:00:53,641 --> 00:00:57,600
to perform vital inspections and repairs inside a sewer tunnel

15
00:00:57,601 --> 00:01:03,100
To insulate him from hazardous gases and materials, he dons a protective suit.

16
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:11,540
Well, the equipment that we use in our sewer inspection work is a full dry suit.

17
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,820
That is a heavy-duty, high-density polyethylene suit.

18
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:19,360
It's puncture-resistant. They're fairly thick.

19
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:21,220
They're chemical-resistant.

20
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:26,860
Now, they can be used in all different types of applications, including sewers, open water dives.

21
00:01:26,861 --> 00:01:32,540
tail races on dams and power plants, both fossil fuel and nuclear as well.

22
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:37,300
We use insulating garments in the cold weather dives.

23
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:43,160
We've got the full face mask that has the underwater communications built in,

24
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:45,840
as well as the surface air supply.

25
00:01:45,841 --> 00:01:51,460
And that forms the basis of the personal protection equipment that we use when we go in the sewers.

26
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:09,560
Eric, can you hear us?

27
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:16,460
He does much of his work in dark, murky water, sometimes navigating by touch more than by sight.

28
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:20,540
We have one engineer diver that goes in.

29
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:26,260
He's going to record all the observations, any structural discontinuities.

30
00:02:26,261 --> 00:02:30,960
going to take measurements that are necessary to perform an engineering analysis on the integrity

31
00:02:30,961 --> 00:02:34,247
of the sewer. We keep our task in mind.

32
00:02:34,248 --> 00:02:38,460
We're there to be looking at the conditions of the

33
00:02:38,461 --> 00:02:43,151
sewer above the waterline, and we're not looking at the liquid itself.

34
00:02:43,152 --> 00:02:44,760
That is not the point of

35
00:02:44,761 --> 00:02:46,413
the inspection.

36
00:02:46,414 --> 00:02:55,560
For obvious reasons, the preferred way to conduct such business is from as far away

37
00:02:55,561 --> 00:02:56,515
as possible.

38
00:02:56,516 --> 00:03:02,640
But in cases where a more hands-on approach is key, that's when Eric goes in.

39
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:07,929
But don't tell him his job stinks. I enjoy this job thoroughly.

40
00:03:07,930 --> 00:03:10,040
The best aspect is we get to be

41
00:03:10,041 --> 00:03:15,240
outside, we get away from the desk and the computer, we get in the fresh air, and we get

42
00:03:15,241 --> 00:03:20,161
to see things most people never have an opportunity to see firsthand.

43
00:03:20,162 --> 00:03:22,300
Human waste removal and sewers

44
00:03:22,301 --> 00:03:24,280
aren't exactly the most pleasant of topics.

45
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:27,760
Perhaps no other essential infrastructure

46
00:03:27,761 --> 00:03:30,120
is so reviled or, at the very least,

47
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:31,920
ignored by the general population.

48
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:35,960
But there's always been a bizarre fascination

49
00:03:35,961 --> 00:03:37,740
with what lurks in the tunnels below.

50
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:42,440
Mythicized in film and literature

51
00:03:42,441 --> 00:03:43,900
with tales of romance,

52
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:46,580
monsters, and mayhem,

53
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,860
sewers have always captured our imaginations

54
00:03:49,861 --> 00:03:51,520
and fears.

55
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:57,300
Rats and giant alligators are among the creatures believed to dwell in the sewers.

56
00:03:57,301 --> 00:04:01,300
But are the stories fact or fiction?

57
00:04:01,301 --> 00:04:05,300
We've been chased out of sewers by alligators in Florida.

58
00:04:05,301 --> 00:04:12,300
The crocodile was found in the network in a sewer about 30 years ago.

59
00:04:12,301 --> 00:04:17,300
And 10 years ago, this animal was still alive.

60
00:04:17,301 --> 00:04:21,300
It was in a zoo in the city of France.

61
00:04:21,301 --> 00:04:26,800
There have been sewers discovered in certain southern states that are just basically plugged up with snakes.

62
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:31,520
The flow can't get down the sewer because there's so many snakes in the system.

63
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:40,940
Just a quick word on the animal we find in great numbers in the sewers, the common rat.

64
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:45,760
It's estimated that in the sewer, there are two rats for every Parisian on the surface.

65
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:48,020
We learn to love them.

66
00:04:48,021 --> 00:04:51,440
They take part in the work.

67
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:54,380
They eliminate residues in the sewers.

68
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:57,460
They consume whatever is consumable to them.

69
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,840
They are also part of the security system.

70
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:04,780
If you find yourself in the sewer and you see rats panicking,

71
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:08,820
chances are there is the presence of gas or some other danger around.

72
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:11,440
We listen to them and we follow them.

73
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:17,260
Despite the occasional creepy creature, what's mostly in the sewers is sewage.

74
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:21,960
Millions of gallons of the gross smelly stuff that flows beneath our feet.

75
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:30,500
But the earliest sewers built over 5,000 years ago were primarily intended to carry stormwater out of the streets to prevent flooding.

76
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:35,320
Washing human waste away as well was just a happy byproduct.

77
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:45,320
Historians believe that the first sewers were probably created by the Indus River societies, which is modern-day Pakistan.

78
00:05:45,321 --> 00:05:48,260
One city that comes to mind is Mohenjo-Daro.

79
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:51,620
And this was about 3,500 years BCE.

80
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:55,560
Learning from those who came before them,

81
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:58,480
the ancient Romans employed elaborate drainage systems

82
00:05:58,481 --> 00:06:00,160
for storm and wastewater carriage.

83
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:06,660
Around 800 BC, the industrious Romans built public latrines

84
00:06:06,661 --> 00:06:10,320
that often used water recycled from the adjacent public baths

85
00:06:10,321 --> 00:06:11,540
to move human waste.

86
00:06:11,541 --> 00:06:16,820
built on a slant to make use of the natural force of gravity

87
00:06:16,821 --> 00:06:23,420
miles of stone sewer drains carried the waste toward a main drain which emptied into the tiber

88
00:06:23,421 --> 00:06:30,860
river this forward thinking could have been the start of something great but by the fourth century

89
00:06:30,861 --> 00:06:38,000
sanitation took a tremendous leap backwards into the dark ages after the roman empire fell

90
00:06:38,001 --> 00:06:41,400
People forgot about infrastructure, forgot about public works.

91
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:43,740
It was more an age of survival.

92
00:06:44,200 --> 00:06:51,880
And if you lived in those times, you probably had a chance of living to 30, 35 years of age and not much beyond.

93
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:58,900
And the reason for your death would not have been war, but it would have been plagues and sickness that would have caused the deaths.

94
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:03,560
One of the area's hardest hit by disease was Paris, France.

95
00:07:03,561 --> 00:07:08,660
built in the ruins of the Roman city of Lutetia around the year 360.

96
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:16,180
Long before it was the city of light, Paris was a city of blight.

97
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,220
The early sanitation system was hardly a system at all.

98
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:26,660
So the Parisians essentially dealt with garbage by using a process called toute la rue,

99
00:07:26,661 --> 00:07:31,220
which basically means that they were discarding everything by throwing it out the window.

100
00:07:31,221 --> 00:07:40,420
Generally they would call out Garalo as a warning and essentially just then throw it out onto the streets.

101
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:46,940
Now the streets themselves were not paved, which means that all of the refuse would essentially mix with the mud

102
00:07:46,941 --> 00:07:55,000
and turn into a rather bad-smelling edible muck that was consumed by dogs, pigs occasionally as well.

103
00:07:55,980 --> 00:08:06,160
Wastewater, stormwater, all the waters went to the street and they stayed in the street.

104
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:13,640
If the slope was enough, this water flowed to the Seine River.

105
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:19,720
In the late 1200s, to combat the foul odors and nuisance of street refuse,

106
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:24,400
some roads were paved and had a gully for wastes running down the middle.

107
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:32,200
But less sewage in the street meant more sewage washing directly into the Seine, the source

108
00:08:32,201 --> 00:08:35,980
of the city's drinking water.

109
00:08:35,981 --> 00:08:40,980
Rivers like the Seine have a natural ability to break down and assimilate harmful microorganisms

110
00:08:40,981 --> 00:08:44,320
found in human and animal waste.

111
00:08:44,321 --> 00:08:48,880
But as a growing population pushed the limits of a delicate ecosystem, the Seine became

112
00:08:48,881 --> 00:08:51,360
overwhelmed.

113
00:08:51,361 --> 00:08:56,463
consumed the infested water, disease spread, and many thousands died.

114
00:08:56,464 --> 00:08:57,720
Engineers look to

115
00:08:57,721 --> 00:09:02,800
a potential solution to the city's sanitation needs, cesspools.

116
00:09:02,801 --> 00:09:08,272
Cesspools are essentially like septic tanks. They are meant to hold human sewage.

117
00:09:08,273 --> 00:09:08,973
Cesspools

118
00:09:08,974 --> 00:09:15,360
were mandated in 1539 as a response to the plagues that were essentially sweeping through

119
00:09:15,361 --> 00:09:16,360
town.

120
00:09:16,361 --> 00:09:23,360
A cylindrical pit, several feet in diameter and several feet deep, was dug in the ground.

121
00:09:23,361 --> 00:09:28,360
The inner wall would be lined with stones.

122
00:09:28,361 --> 00:09:34,360
Liquid wastes would seep out through the stones, and solids would build up inside the cesspool.

123
00:09:34,361 --> 00:09:41,360
While there was some breakdown of the solids, if not cleaned regularly, the excreta would overflow into the streets,

124
00:09:41,361 --> 00:09:45,360
and ultimately end up in the river just the same.

125
00:09:45,361 --> 00:09:52,840
After several malodorous years, French authorities established the Night Soil Program, an early

126
00:09:52,841 --> 00:09:55,800
foray into waste removal and recycling.

127
00:09:55,801 --> 00:10:01,480
Finally, the offensive material that collected in cesspools would be put to good use.

128
00:10:01,481 --> 00:10:08,680
In the 1650s, it was decided that all of this untapped resource would essentially be transformed

129
00:10:08,681 --> 00:10:11,900
into what was called Night Soil.

130
00:10:11,901 --> 00:10:21,940
They would produce fertilizer by essentially composting the human sewage and also creating saltpeter, which was used in the manufacture of gunpowder.

131
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:29,800
When plumbing arrived on the scene in the 1700s, it was intended less for sanitation and more for beautification.

132
00:10:31,420 --> 00:10:33,580
Public fountains overflowed with water.

133
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:39,160
People saw them as good places to quench their thirst and relieve themselves.

134
00:10:39,161 --> 00:10:43,160
Right now we're in front of the Fontaine de Saint-Innocent.

135
00:10:43,161 --> 00:10:48,160
This was one of the principal water supplies for central Paris.

136
00:10:48,161 --> 00:10:53,160
When they started having the cholera epidemics in the 19th century,

137
00:10:53,161 --> 00:11:00,160
these epidemics essentially were occurring based on tainted water supply sources.

138
00:11:00,161 --> 00:11:04,160
And it was very quickly found that the number of deaths increased proportionally

139
00:11:04,161 --> 00:11:07,160
to their distance from these water supplies.

140
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:18,303
To combat epidemics which were quickly spreading across Europe, engineers had to create centralized systems to convey sewage away from populated areas quickly and

141
00:11:18,304 --> 00:11:19,004
efficiently.

142
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:32,795
In Paris in the early 1850s, during the reign of Napoleon III, Baron Georges Haussmann, prefect of the Seine, hired chief engineer Eugène Belgron to oversee the

143
00:11:32,796 --> 00:11:35,100
development of the city's sewers.

144
00:11:35,101 --> 00:11:42,100
Belgron designed a network of gravity-fed sewers that made maximum use of the land's natural slope.

145
00:11:42,101 --> 00:11:49,100
Teams of men with picks and shovels dug open trenches for the smaller, shallow sewers.

146
00:11:49,101 --> 00:11:55,100
Deeper lines were tunneled much the same way, but entirely underground, without trenches.

147
00:11:55,101 --> 00:12:03,100
Lines ran directly beneath the streets, and street signs were posted on the tunnel walls, so workers could navigate the system from below.

148
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:18,120
Between 1850 and the end of the century, they built up to 40 kilometers a year.

149
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:23,800
Belgrane also designed cleaning machines to prevent tunnel blockages.

150
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:28,220
One of these inventions was a giant hollow wooden ball,

151
00:12:28,840 --> 00:12:31,700
slightly smaller in diameter than the sewer pipe it cleaned.

152
00:12:31,701 --> 00:12:35,880
The ball was dropped in through a shaft upstream.

153
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:40,720
It rolled down the pipe, restricting flow and creating water pressure behind it

154
00:12:40,721 --> 00:12:43,060
that moved solid waste toward a catch chamber.

155
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:47,700
From there, the waste would be pumped out and transported away from the city.

156
00:12:49,180 --> 00:12:53,440
Another of Belgrane's innovations is still in use today, the flushing boat.

157
00:12:53,441 --> 00:13:01,820
It is used to clean the sewers and to remove the sound from the bottom of the sewers.

158
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:13,600
As a testament to the quality of Belgrin's work, most of the original Paris sewers are still in operation today and serve an estimated 3 million people.

159
00:13:15,300 --> 00:13:22,340
And while they may not rank with the more notable attractions, tours of the Paris sewers have always drawn curious visitors.

160
00:13:23,440 --> 00:13:37,440
But the early Parisians and other Europeans who made their way to the new world, quickly

161
00:13:43,300 --> 00:13:47,500
found that issues of public sanitation would follow.

162
00:13:47,501 --> 00:13:52,500
Across the Atlantic, it was going to be a real case of engineering mind over organic

163
00:13:52,501 --> 00:13:53,201
matter.

164
00:13:53,202 --> 00:13:59,500
If laid end to end, the tunnels of the Paris sewer system would stretch all the

165
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:06,860
way to Istanbul, over 1,300 miles. Sewers will return on Modern Marvels, here on the

166
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:11,540
History Channel.

167
00:14:16,380 --> 00:14:20,380
One of the oldest cities in the United States is Boston, Massachusetts.

168
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:26,300
Here, the contributions of our founding fathers are still evident today.

169
00:14:27,940 --> 00:14:33,580
This is true even underground, where about 70% of the original sewers remain in service.

170
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:37,780
This is the original brickwork dating back to the 1880s.

171
00:14:38,140 --> 00:14:39,840
As you can see, it's still in really good shape.

172
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:42,460
It's a testament to the work that they did back then.

173
00:14:44,180 --> 00:14:45,960
It's built on a rock foundation.

174
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:48,680
As it gets towards the bottom, it's rounded off.

175
00:14:48,681 --> 00:14:52,700
that gives a nice smooth path for the floater to be carried in

176
00:14:52,701 --> 00:14:55,440
and minimizes the amount of sediment buildup in the pipe.

177
00:14:59,760 --> 00:15:04,380
Present day Boston is a far cry from the way it started out, geographically speaking.

178
00:15:05,420 --> 00:15:09,200
Boston was first settled in 1630 as a town,

179
00:15:09,860 --> 00:15:14,000
a rather small town, on three mountains of a small landmass.

180
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:15,920
They called it Tri-Mountain at the time.

181
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:20,940
For their sanitation purposes, they used privies, either in their cellars or in their backyards.

182
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:23,380
Their biggest problem, though, was rainwater.

183
00:15:23,820 --> 00:15:29,020
So they'd get together as a group, and they would build their own drains and make it go away.

184
00:15:29,440 --> 00:15:31,840
That was the whole theory of sewers, go away.

185
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:37,240
But water wasn't the only thing early Bostonians wanted to go away.

186
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:43,800
Perhaps a chicken head or an old pot or something you just wanted to get rid of would be dumped in the drain.

187
00:15:44,300 --> 00:15:46,960
Now, although they prohibited the actual solids,

188
00:15:47,380 --> 00:15:49,240
we know for a fact that the solids got in there.

189
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:52,600
Thanks to good old gravity,

190
00:15:53,180 --> 00:15:55,360
the whole mess trickled down right into the harbor.

191
00:15:56,580 --> 00:15:58,100
Since no one drank from the ocean,

192
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:01,300
Boston was largely able to avoid the unpleasantries

193
00:16:01,301 --> 00:16:04,240
and diseases that plagued Parisians for years.

194
00:16:05,020 --> 00:16:06,660
Still, this gravity-powered solution

195
00:16:06,661 --> 00:16:09,120
wasn't too pleasant for the people at the bottom of the hill.

196
00:16:11,640 --> 00:16:13,600
The area continued to expand,

197
00:16:13,800 --> 00:16:17,620
using landfill to push the boundaries of an almost island-like city.

198
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:23,140
As the community grew, drainage systems were extended to the new tide line.

199
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:27,880
Well, they did it at their own cost, so they were kind of shoddy construction.

200
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:30,860
No one really supervised what they did.

201
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:36,960
And what it did was it brought the sewage down to a point where it would hit the tide lines,

202
00:16:37,640 --> 00:16:41,320
and it would sit there, and at high tide, it couldn't get out of the pipe.

203
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:46,460
At low tide, it would spill out of the pipe, partially settling in the pipe,

204
00:16:46,940 --> 00:16:49,840
and sit on the flats of the river and the harbor.

205
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:53,200
And that was an absolute delight during the summer.

206
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:55,120
An unbelievable stench.

207
00:16:56,840 --> 00:17:00,140
The advent of the modern water closet didn't help matters either.

208
00:17:01,380 --> 00:17:04,900
All that extra water and raw sewage dumped right into the drains.

209
00:17:04,901 --> 00:17:11,900
In 1876, after examining the successes and failures of European sewer systems,

210
00:17:11,901 --> 00:17:15,900
Massachusetts state legislators approved the construction of the projected

211
00:17:15,901 --> 00:17:18,900
$3 million Boston Main drainage system.

212
00:17:18,901 --> 00:17:21,900
It was designed as a combined sewer system.

213
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:29,900
Both stormwater runoff and wastewater from homes would enter the system via drains.

214
00:17:29,901 --> 00:17:36,900
But before hitting the shore, the sewage would be intercepted by a large brick-lined sewer

215
00:17:36,901 --> 00:17:38,900
running around the perimeter of the city.

216
00:17:38,901 --> 00:17:44,900
All the collected water would travel through this single gravity-fed conduit to Moon Island,

217
00:17:44,901 --> 00:17:51,900
far away from Boston's populated area, where it would be held for release with the outgoing tide into deeper water.

218
00:17:51,901 --> 00:17:57,900
Building anything around or under a bustling city is hard enough,

219
00:17:57,901 --> 00:18:01,900
But doing it over 100 years ago was painstaking.

220
00:18:01,901 --> 00:18:06,900
Back in the 1880s, they would have used a lot of manpower, picks, shovels.

221
00:18:06,901 --> 00:18:08,900
They would have excavated by hand.

222
00:18:08,901 --> 00:18:11,900
Now, during the process, the city was a very busy place.

223
00:18:11,901 --> 00:18:15,900
Railroads were prevalent, and you couldn't shut down railroads to build sewers under them.

224
00:18:15,901 --> 00:18:20,900
So one of the superintendents, a man named Carson, developed a machine

225
00:18:20,901 --> 00:18:26,900
that could actually build the sewer while letting traffic work in the area.

226
00:18:26,901 --> 00:18:28,860
The machine was 200 feet long.

227
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:31,160
It weighed 10 tons.

228
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:34,920
At the front, the people would be digging by hand and shovel.

229
00:18:35,660 --> 00:18:37,680
They'd be putting the dirt into buckets.

230
00:18:38,380 --> 00:18:40,080
